findings.body:
The test helper AssertEvents.isAccessTokenId is broken in two ways and does not verify the grant-type segment encoded in access-token ids, so every test that relies on it (e.g. lines 145, 173, 182, 244 asserting AuthorizationCode 'ac', Device 'dg', RefreshToken 'rt', CIBA 'ci' grant shortcuts) is a false-positive.

(1) WRONG SLICE: The helper extracts items[0].substring(3, 5) but the encoder (DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.encodeTokenId / getTokenContextFromTokenId) builds the prefix as sessionType(2) + tokenType(2) + grantType(2) + ':' + rawId, and decodes the grant shortcut at substring(4, 6). The grant segment is at positions 4-5 (0-indexed), not 3-4. substring(3, 5) reads the 2nd char of the token-type and the 1st char of the grant-type, which can never equal any 2-char grant shortcut like 'ac', 'dg', 'rt', 'ci'.

(2) INVERTED CONDITION: matchesSafely does `if (items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;` then falls through to `return isUUID().matches(items[1])`. It returns false when the slice EQUALS the expected grant, and returns true (pass) whenever the slice does NOT equal the expected grant and the UUID part is valid. A TypeSafeMatcher is supposed to return true when the match succeeds. Because the wrong slice never equals the expected grant shortcut, the condition is effectively never triggered, so the matcher degrades to `isUUID().matches(items[1])` alone — the grant-type segment is never validated.

Concrete consequence: Tests for O4 and O9-O11 (grant-type encoding for authorization-code, device, refresh-token, ciba grants) pass regardless of which grant shortcut was actually encoded in the token id, so regressions that swap or corrupt the grant segment would not be caught.

CHANGED END (AssertEvents.java:476-492):
    public static Matcher<String> isAccessTokenId(String expectedGrantShortcut) {
        return new TypeSafeMatcher<String>() {
            @Override
            protected boolean matchesSafely(String item) {
                String[] items = item.split(":");
                if (items.length != 2) return false;
                // Grant type shortcut starts at character 4th char and is 2-chars long
                if (items[0].substring(3, 5).equals(expectedGrantShortcut)) return false;
                return isUUID().matches(items[1]);
            }
            ...
        };
    }

RELIED-ON END (DefaultTokenContextEncoderProvider.java:74-81, 114-117):
    // decode:
    if (encodedContext.length() != 6) { throw ... }
    String stShortcut = encodedContext.substring(0, 2);
    String ttShortcut = encodedContext.substring(2, 4);
    String gtShortcut = encodedContext.substring(4, 6);   // grant shortcut is at 4-6
    // encode:
    return tokenContext.getSessionType().getShortcut() +
           tokenContext.getTokenType().getShortcut() +
           grantShort +
           ':' + tokenContext.getRawTokenId();

The encoder places the grant shortcut at index 4-5 of the prefix; the helper reads index 3-4 and inverts the equality test. The two ends disagree.

